le for fruits and vegetables; beverage for drinks; grain/tea for nuts, tea, grains, and dried goods. This flexibility makes the variable compartment a versatile storage solution that adapts to different household needs.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Key Storage Components: Drawers and Tray\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The variable compartment includes two types of internal components: the variable compartment drawer (变温室抽屉) and the variable compartment tray (变温室托盘). Typically, each refrigerator comes with 2 drawers and 1 tray. [Data: Entities (424, 425); Relationships (486, 504, 476, 490, 503, 491)] The drawers are designed for storing food at specific mode settings (e.g., fish/meat at -1°C, fruit/vegetable at 2°C, soft freezing at -3°C, or freezing), while the tray serves as a general platform. These components are integral to organizing food and maintaining the compartment's temperature zones.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Specialized Drawers: Moisture-Fresh and Dry-Fresh\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond the variable compartment, the refrigerator features two specialized drawers in the main compartment: the water-run-fresh drawer (水润臻鲜抽屉) and the treasure-dry-fresh drawer (珍品干鲜抽屉). The water-run-fresh drawer uses a sealing design and ventilation holes to maintain high humidity, ideal for fruits and vegetables. [Data: Entities (427, 744, 743); Relationships (509, 861, 860)] The dry-fresh drawer uses ventilation holes and air ducts to maintain a low-humidity, dry environment, perfect for storing dry goods like tea, dried sea cucumber, and dried fruits. [Data: Entities (423, 745); Relationships (862, 863)] These drawers complement the variable compartment by addressing specific humidity needs.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Food-Specific Storage Recommendations Across Components\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The community includes detailed mappings between food types and recommended storage locations. For example, meat, fish, steak, and bacon are best stored in fish/meat mode; fruits and vegetables in fruit/vegetable mode or the water-run-fresh drawer; beverages in beverage mode; and dry goods (nuts, grains, tea, dried sea cucumber, dried fruits) in grain/tea mode or the dry-fresh drawer. [Data: Entities (459, 460, 461, 462, 463, 464, 466, 467, 441, 442, 443, 465, 458); Relationships (521, 522, 523, 524, 525, 526, 498, 499, 500, 531, 532, 529, 530, 520)] The water-run-fresh drawer is specifically highlighted for high-humidity items, while the dry-fresh drawer is for low-humidity items. This structured guidance helps users preserve food quality.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Safety Warning: Glass Bottle Breakage Risk\",\n            \"explanation\": \"A critical safety insight is the warning about glass bottle breakage: glass bottles stored in the variable compartment can break when set to freezing or soft freezing modes, potentially causing injury. [Data: Entities (679); Relationships (788)] This applies directly to modes that cause freezing (below 0°C), highlighting a need for user awareness and caution to prevent accidents. This warning is a key risk factor in the community's impact rating.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Interconnection Between Component Design and Mode Selection\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The relationships show a systematic integration: mode selection (e.g., soft freezing, fish/meat) is applied to the variable compartment, which then dictates the appropriate use of drawers and tray. [Data: Relationships (780, 781, 782, 783, 784, 785)] The drawers are physically linked to specific mode settings, as seen in relationships linking the drawer to fish/meat, fruit/vegetable, soft freezing, and freezing modes. [Data: Relationships (504, 505, 503, 490)] This design ensures that users can match food types with optimal temperature and humidity conditions for extended freshness.\"\